::CW_U02_L02_GP_Q01::Which sentence is written in first person point of view?{
~A. Lena opened the drawer and held her breath.#Incorrect. This is third person because the narrator uses Lena's name.
~B. She opened the drawer and held her breath.#Incorrect. This is third person because the narrator uses she.
=C. I opened the drawer and held my breath.#Correct. The narrator uses I and my, so the narrator is inside the story.
~D. They opened the drawer and held their breath.#Incorrect. This is third person because the narrator uses they and their.
}
::CW_U02_L02_GP_Q02::Which choice correctly explains third person limited point of view?{
=A. The narrator uses names or third person pronouns and stays close to one character's thoughts.#Correct. Third person limited is outside the story but limited to one focus character's experience.
~B. The narrator uses I and tells only personal experience.#Incorrect. That describes first person point of view.
~C. The narrator directly reveals every character's thoughts at any time.#Incorrect. That describes omniscient narration, not limited narration.
~D. The narrator never reveals any thoughts or feelings.#Incorrect. Third person limited can reveal one character's thoughts and feelings.
}
::CW_U02_L02_GP_Q03::Which sentence creates a confusing point-of-view shift?{
~A. I watched Jada fold the note and slide it into her pocket.#Incorrect. This stays in first person and reports observable action.
~B. I watched Jada fold the note, and her fingers shook.#Incorrect. This stays in first person because the narrator observes Jada's fingers.
~C. I watched Jada fold the note and guessed she was nervous.#Incorrect. This stays in first person because the narrator makes a guess instead of claiming direct knowledge.
=D. I watched Jada fold the note, and she knew I would tell everyone.#Correct. A first person narrator cannot directly know what Jada knows unless the story gives a reason.
}
::CW_U02_L02_GP_Q04::Why does point of view matter in narrative writing?{
~A. It only changes pronouns, not meaning.#Incorrect. Point of view changes knowledge, voice, distance, and reader trust.
=B. It controls who tells the story and what the reader can know.#Correct. Point of view shapes the reader's access to information and experience.
~C. It allows a writer to ignore narrator knowledge.#Incorrect. Point of view requires control of what the narrator can know.
~D. It matters only in poetry, not narrative writing.#Incorrect. Point of view is central to narrative writing.
}
::CW_U02_L02_GP_Q05::Which revision keeps first person point of view consistent? Draft: "I saw Marco hide the map. He was afraid I would take it."{
~A. I saw Marco hide the map. He was afraid I would take it.#Incorrect. This still claims Marco's private fear directly.
~B. Marco hid the map because he was afraid I would take it.#Incorrect. This shifts away from the first person narrator and still states Marco's fear as fact.
=C. I saw Marco hide the map. His hands moved quickly, and he would not look at me.#Correct. The narrator reports observable action instead of directly claiming Marco's private thought.
~D. Marco saw me see the map, and I knew he was afraid.#Incorrect. This remains confusing because it mixes knowledge claims.
}
